component SigilImplementationOwnership {
  goal {
    Provide the shared core query that returns implementation targets linked to a Sigil contract from implementation comments.

    Keep implementation-to-contract ownership discovery deterministic and reusable for CLI and LSP without writing annotations into Sigil source.
  }

  interface {
    OwnedImplementationLookup {
      ownedImplementationTargetsFor(resolvedWorkspace, implementationSources, componentName, conceptName?) returns an owned-implementation projection or undefined

      The query scans implementation comments that link entrypoint definitions or agent-facing Markdown back to the selected Sigil component and optional concept.
    }

    OwnedImplementationProjection {
      includes the owning contract, optional concept scope, normalized implementation targets, and diagnostics
    }

    OwnedImplementationTarget {
      each target identifies one owned implementation artifact

      each target preserves the artifact kind, file path, and optional symbol identity when available
    }

    ImplementationAnnotation {
      identifies a relation, repository-relative Sigil path, component name, and optional concept name

      the containing implementation file and adjacent entrypoint definition identify the owned implementation target
    }
  }
}

expand SigilImplementationOwnership {
  logic {
    OwnedImplementationProjection {
      scan supplied implementation sources for supported ownership comments

      resolve each annotation target against the selected Sigil workspace

      normalize code, tests, and agent-facing workflow Markdown into one shared result shape

      derive symbol-level targets from annotated language entrypoint definitions and use file-level targets for Markdown
    }
  }

  constraints {
    AnnotationStorage {
      Ownership annotations exist only in implementation comments and never in Sigil source.

      Each annotation points from an implementation artifact to a repository-relative Sigil path, component, and optional concept.
    }

    AnnotationPlacement {
      A source annotation is immediately associated with a language entrypoint definition such as a class, function, method, interface, struct, or equivalent stable definition.

      One annotation uses the language's single-line comment form.

      Multiple annotations use the language's multiline comment form.

      Markdown annotations use HTML comments.

      JSON annotations are outside the current scope.
    }

    ImplementationTargetScope {
      v1 includes source code and tests with supported comment syntax.

      v1 includes Markdown only for agent-facing instructions or workflow documents.

      Sigil sources, JSON, general prose documentation, configs, and non-text artifacts remain out of scope.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      Resolve source annotations to their adjacent entrypoint definition.

      Resolve agent-facing Markdown annotations at file level.

      Ignore ambiguous or detached annotations and return diagnostics rather than inferring ownership.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    AnnotationStorage {
      Decision: Store ownership links in implementation comments that point back to governing Sigil components and concepts.

      Scope: Governs shared CLI and LSP ownership discovery for source code, tests, and agent-facing Markdown; excludes annotations inside Sigil and JSON support.

      Trade-offs: Implementation-local links are visible where code changes occur, but scanners must understand language comment and entrypoint syntax.

      Design issues addressed: Sigil contracts should remain implementation-independent while code retains a reviewable link to its governing rationale.

      Consequences: Ownership discovery scans implementation artifacts, and Sigil files contain no implementation-link annotations.

      Revisit when: A language cannot expose a stable comment or entrypoint association.
    }

    AnnotationPlacement {
      Decision: Attach annotations to stable language entrypoint definitions, using single-line comments for one link and multiline comments for multiple links.

      Scope: Governs source annotation placement and comment form; Markdown uses HTML comments and remains file-scoped.

      Trade-offs: Entrypoint placement improves symbol navigation but requires language-aware scanning.

      Consequences: Classes, functions, methods, interfaces, structs, and equivalent definitions become linkable ownership entrypoints.

      Revisit when: Supported languages require declaration metadata instead of comments.
    }

    ScopeSelection {
      Decision: Allow an optional concept scope so a contract can return all owned targets or just the subset governed by one concept.

      Scope: Governs query scoping within one contract; excludes cross-contract aggregation and transitive ownership inference.

      Trade-offs: Concept scoping increases precision without forcing separate query shapes, but callers must supply the correct concept when they need a narrower view.

      Consequences: The same API serves whole-contract and concept-specific ownership views.

      Revisit when: Concept scoping proves too coarse or too fine for routine use.
    }

    ImplementationTargetScope {
      Decision: Limit v1 implementation targets to source code and tests with supported comments plus agent-facing instruction or workflow Markdown.

      Scope: Governs which repository artifacts may appear in owned-implementation results; excludes Sigil sources, JSON, general prose documentation, configs, and non-text artifacts.

      Trade-offs: The scope captures behavior-shaping Markdown that matters to agent workflows, but intentionally leaves ordinary documentation and unsupported artifact formats out.

      Consequences: The feature stays focused on comment-bearing implementation material while still covering instruction docs.

      Revisit when: Other artifact classes become meaningful implementation evidence.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      Decision: Resolve source annotations to adjacent language entrypoint definitions and resolve agent-facing Markdown at file level.

      Scope: Governs how owned implementation targets are normalized inside the query result; excludes detached-annotation inference and human approval.

      Trade-offs: Language-aware entrypoint resolution improves navigation, while Markdown remains useful through file-level links.

      Consequences: The same query can link source and test entrypoints plus file-level Markdown targets without guessing ambiguous ownership.

      Revisit when: Supported languages need declaration metadata or Markdown requires section-level targets.
    }
  }

  cases {
    OwnedImplementationLookup {
      A TypeScript line comment immediately before one function links that function to a Sigil component or concept.

      A language multiline comment contains multiple ownership annotations for one class, method, interface, struct, or equivalent entrypoint.

      An HTML comment links an agent-facing Markdown workflow file to a Sigil component or concept.

      JSON content is ignored.
    }

    TargetResolution {
      A source annotation adjacent to a supported entrypoint produces a symbol-level implementation target.

      A Markdown instruction comment produces a file-level implementation target.

      A detached or ambiguous annotation produces a diagnostic instead of an inferred target.
    }
  }
}
